WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.114
Welcome to section seven, the GI system and routes of nutrition delivery.

2
00:00:06.114 --> 00:00:15.498
Nutrition is a respiratory topic because the diaphragm is a muscle, and a starved or overfed patient may struggle to wean.

3
00:00:15.498 --> 00:00:24.496
Hess chapter eleven names only three routes of nutrition: oral, enteral, and parenteral, in that order of preference.

4
00:00:24.496 --> 00:00:46.800
In this lesson we will walk through each route, the enteral tube choices, when parenteral nutrition is actually indicated, aspiration precautions during tube feeding, and two high-yield metabolic traps: overfeeding with its respiratory quotient and refeeding syndrome with hypophosphatemia.

5
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.192
Oral is the preferred method of nutrient delivery.

6
00:00:52.192 --> 00:01:01.533
Enteral, in Hess chapter eleven, means feeding-tube delivery through the gut when the patient cannot chew or swallow.

7
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:16.464
Parenteral means any route other than the alimentary canal, essentially an IV, and it is reserved for when the GI tract is not functioning, or when stimulating it would make things worse.

8
00:01:16.464 --> 00:01:31.474
Oral often falls short in lung disease because of dyspnea while eating, early satiety, reflux, bloating from air swallowing, and side effects of bronchodilators, steroids, and antibiotics.

9
00:01:31.474 --> 00:01:43.530
The rule from Hess: if oral intake falls short despite high-calorie, high-protein supplements and snacks, enteral feeding by tube should be considered.

10
00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.400
Enteral is also the method of choice for ventilated patients.

11
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.693
Enteral access splits by duration.

12
00:01:52.693 --> 00:02:04.180
Nasogastric and orogastric tubes go through the nose or mouth into the stomach, and are used short term, less than three to four weeks.

13
00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:18.560
The Dobhoff is a small-bore flexible NG tube, about four millimeters inside diameter, placed over a stylet with a weighted end that peristalsis carries into the stomach.

14
00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.900
It is usually more comfortable than other NG tubes.

15
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:36.344
NG tubes may be contraindicated in severe reflux, gastroparesis, or high aspiration risk, and Hess chapter thirty-nine warns they can impair the cough reflex.

16
00:02:36.344 --> 00:02:53.617
Percutaneous tubes, such as a PEG placed endoscopically in a sedated patient, go through the skin into the stomach or small intestine for long-term feeding, and can be replaced without another endoscopy.

17
00:02:53.617 --> 00:03:01.275
Comfort order: PEG is more comfortable than nasal tubes, and Dobhoff beats other NG tubes.

18
00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:13.107
A post-pyloric or small-bowel tube goes past the stomach into the duodenum or jejunum, optimally past the ligament of Treitz.

19
00:03:13.107 --> 00:03:25.012
It is chosen when aspiration risk is high or when gastric emptying is poor; the more distal the feeding, presumably the less likely aspiration.

20
00:03:25.012 --> 00:03:32.588
A larger gastric tube can decompress the stomach while the post-pyloric tube delivers feed.

21
00:03:32.588 --> 00:03:51.237
Confirming position is an RT concern: the tip and side hole must sit beyond the gastroesophageal junction, and an improperly positioned feeding tube can enter the airway, so radiographic confirmation is essential before use.

22
00:03:51.237 --> 00:04:03.475
Hess adds that a colorimetric CO2 detector or capnography can catch accidental tracheal placement; a color change means the tube is in the trachea.

23
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:16.678
Parenteral nutrition is reserved for when the GI tract is not functioning, or when stimulating the gut or pancreas would worsen the patient.

24
00:04:16.678 --> 00:04:25.175
Hess lists severe pancreatitis, GI fistulas, short bowel syndrome, prolonged ileus, and some cancers.

25
00:04:25.175 --> 00:04:31.653
It should not be started if support is expected to last less than seven days.

26
00:04:31.653 --> 00:04:40.402
Access needs a central or peripheral venous catheter, and Hess says central access is usually preferred.

27
00:04:40.402 --> 00:05:01.434
The RCP 203 deck explains the two lines: a peripheral line is a short catheter in a small vein for short-term fluids or non-irritating medications, while a central line sits in a large vein such as the jugular, subclavian, or femoral, or an arm PICC.

28
00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:07.575
It is designed for longer-term use and lists IV nutrition among its uses.

29
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.334
Pause and think.

30
00:05:10.334 --> 00:05:16.170
Even when parenteral nutrition is clearly indicated, it carries risks.

31
00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:27.925
What happens to the gut when we bypass it for days, and what carbon dioxide and electrolyte problems should the RT watch for when TPN starts?

32
00:05:28.833 --> 00:05:34.833
[6-second practice pause]

33
00:05:35.333 --> 00:05:49.326
Bypassing the gut leaves it unstimulated, which can lead to mucosal atrophy, weakens the gut barrier, and may raise the risk of bacterial contamination.

34
00:05:49.326 --> 00:06:08.105
On the respiratory side, dextrose-heavy TPN raises oxygen use and CO2 production, and respiratory acidosis during mechanical ventilation has been reported within hours after starting TPN from overfeeding.

35
00:06:08.105 --> 00:06:21.453
Hess chapter five adds that parenteral nutrition with carbohydrate overload drives phosphate into cells and is a named cause of hypophosphatemia.

36
00:06:21.453 --> 00:06:28.633
So the RT watches ventilation, blood gas, and serum phosphate when TPN starts.

37
00:06:30.033 --> 00:06:46.801
Enteral uses a functioning gut, so nutrients travel through the portal system to the liver, which may enhance immune competence, maintain mucosal integrity, preserve normal flora, and stimulate secretory IgA.

38
00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:52.122
It is safer and considerably less expensive, though not risk-free.

39
00:06:52.122 --> 00:07:00.183
Parenteral bypasses the gut, so the gut barrier weakens and infection risk rises, and it costs more.

40
00:07:00.183 --> 00:07:10.905
Hess's key point is simple: enteral is preferred because it carries fewer infection risks, may protect the GI mucosa, and costs less.

41
00:07:10.905 --> 00:07:23.158
Hess chapter thirty-nine adds that trauma patients receiving enteral nutrition for five to seven days had lower sepsis rates than those given IV fluids.

42
00:07:24.567 --> 00:07:39.612
Hess clinical practice guideline eleven-dash-one says to start early enteral nutrition within twenty-four to forty-eight hours in a critically ill patient who cannot maintain volitional intake.

43
00:07:39.612 --> 00:07:41.794
Use enteral over parenteral.

44
00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:48.108
In most critically ill patients it is acceptable to start enteral in the stomach.

45
00:07:48.108 --> 00:07:54.968
Withhold enteral during hemodynamic instability until the patient is fully resuscitated.

46
00:07:54.968 --> 00:08:14.378
For high-risk or severely malnourished patients, advance toward goal over twenty-four to forty-eight hours while watching for refeeding syndrome, aiming for more than eighty percent of goal energy and protein within forty-eight to seventy-two hours.

47
00:08:14.378 --> 00:08:25.136
Protein target: one point two to two point zero grams per kilogram actual body weight per day, possibly higher in burn or multiple trauma.

48
00:08:25.136 --> 00:08:33.867
Energy target when indirect calorimetry is unavailable: twenty-five to thirty kilocalories per kilogram per day.

49
00:08:35.267 --> 00:08:44.548
In all intubated ICU patients receiving enteral nutrition, elevate the head of the bed thirty to forty-five degrees.

50
00:08:44.548 --> 00:08:53.269
Hess chapter thirty-six's ventilator bundle says up to forty-five degrees, especially during enteral feeding.

51
00:08:53.269 --> 00:09:09.111
The RCP one-oh-five Procedures deck notes that studies disagree on the exact angle, and that elevation should be done unless contraindicated, for example after neurosurgery or in severe hypotension.

52
00:09:09.111 --> 00:09:27.833
Beyond the head of bed, divert feeding post-pyloric in high-risk patients, switch from bolus to continuous infusion for intolerant patients, and consider prokinetics such as metoclopramide or erythromycin when aspiration risk is high.

53
00:09:27.833 --> 00:09:34.314
Do not use blue food coloring or glucose oxidase strips as markers of aspiration.

54
00:09:34.314 --> 00:09:47.436
Gastric residual volumes should not be used routinely, and feeds should not be held for residuals under five hundred milliliters without other signs of intolerance.

55
00:09:47.436 --> 00:09:53.917
Before postural drainage, stop tube feeds one hour before and during the therapy.

56
00:09:55.333 --> 00:10:05.673
The respiratory quotient, RQ, equals VCO2 divided by VO2, the ratio of carbon dioxide produced to oxygen consumed.

57
00:10:05.673 --> 00:10:19.188
A measured RQ should fall in the physiologic range of zero point six seven to one point three; critically ill patients sit near zero point nine zero.

58
00:10:19.188 --> 00:10:32.158
Pure carbohydrate burns at an RQ of one point zero, protein near zero point eight, fat near zero point seven one, alcohol zero point six seven.

59
00:10:32.158 --> 00:10:37.419
Higher RQ means more CO2 produced per unit of oxygen used.

60
00:10:37.419 --> 00:10:54.108
Overfeeding carbohydrate drives lipogenesis and extra CO2, which raises ventilatory demand and can precipitate respiratory failure or prolong weaning in a patient with limited reserve.

61
00:10:55.533 --> 00:11:12.404
The decks favor fat-based nutrition for ventilated and CO2-retaining patients: fat supplies more kilocalories with less CO2, and the diet should contain at least forty percent of its kilocalories from fat.

62
00:11:12.404 --> 00:11:27.300
Hess's pulmonary-disease formulas give forty to fifty-five percent of calories from fat and less than forty percent from carbohydrate, at one point five kilocalories per milliliter.

63
00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:48.862
But Hess's clinical practice guideline eleven-dash-one suggests NOT using specialty high-fat, low-carbohydrate formulas in ICU patients with acute respiratory failure, and suggests fluid-restricted, energy-dense formulas instead, especially with volume overload.

64
00:11:48.862 --> 00:11:57.585
The concept the decks test is that more carbohydrate means a higher RQ and more CO2, so avoid overfeeding.

65
00:11:57.585 --> 00:12:04.333
If a question asks what the current ICU guideline recommends, answer with the CPG.

66
00:12:05.733 --> 00:12:10.393
Starvation and hypercatabolism cannibalize the diaphragm.

67
00:12:10.393 --> 00:12:24.698
Protein-calorie malnutrition hits fast-twitch fibers hardest and shows up as a drop in maximum inspiratory and expiratory pressures, vital capacity, and voluntary ventilation.

68
00:12:24.698 --> 00:12:35.406
A malnourished patient may fail a spontaneous breathing trial simply because the diaphragm lacks the strength to sustain breathing.

69
00:12:35.406 --> 00:12:45.787
Malnutrition also impairs cell-mediated immunity and surfactant production, and low albumin raises the risk of pulmonary edema.

70
00:12:45.787 --> 00:12:56.250
Nutritional risk flags are weight loss of ten percent or more over six months, or body weight less than eighty percent of ideal.

71
00:12:56.250 --> 00:13:14.233
The hypercatabolic phase of critical illness can last seven to ten days, and caloric needs may rise by up to one hundred percent; burns may need one and a half to two times, sometimes two to three times, normal calories.

72
00:13:15.633 --> 00:13:16.920
Pause and think.

73
00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:22.952
A severely malnourished patient has just been started on nutrition support.

74
00:13:22.952 --> 00:13:31.154
A cluster of problems can then appear that may precipitate respiratory failure or prolong ventilation.

75
00:13:31.154 --> 00:13:38.633
What is this syndrome called, and which electrolyte does this section tie most closely to it?

76
00:13:39.533 --> 00:13:45.533
[6-second practice pause]

77
00:13:46.033 --> 00:13:57.259
The syndrome is refeeding syndrome, the metabolic disturbance that follows reinstituting nutrition in a starved or malnourished patient.

78
00:13:57.259 --> 00:14:14.924
It brings severe electrolyte imbalance, vitamin deficiency, and hyperglycemia, and can cause arrhythmias, heart failure, pulmonary edema, respiratory failure, diaphragm and intercostal weakness, and a rise in VCO2.

79
00:14:14.924 --> 00:14:25.242
The electrolyte this section focuses on is phosphate; hypophosphatemia decreases ATP and impairs respiratory-muscle function.

80
00:14:25.242 --> 00:14:48.685
The Management of MV deck defines hypophosphatemia as serum phosphate less than one milligram per deciliter, while Hess chapter five gives the adult normal range as two point seven to four point five milligrams per deciliter, so by Hess any value under two point seven is already low.

81
00:14:48.685 --> 00:15:03.708
Advance severely malnourished patients toward goal over twenty-four to forty-eight hours, and in acute respiratory failure monitor serum phosphate closely and replace it when needed.

82
00:15:05.133 --> 00:15:09.675
Three routes, in order: oral, enteral, parenteral.

83
00:15:09.675 --> 00:15:20.665
Enteral tubes split by duration, NG and Dobhoff short term, PEG long term, and post-pyloric when aspiration risk is high.

84
00:15:20.665 --> 00:15:32.291
Parenteral is reserved for a non-functioning gut or when stimulating it would cause harm, and uses a central line when possible.

85
00:15:32.291 --> 00:15:37.378
Head of bed thirty to forty-five degrees during feeding.

86
00:15:37.378 --> 00:15:55.998
Overfeeding carbohydrate raises the RQ and CO2 and may prolong ventilation; the decks favor fat-based formulas, but the current CPG advises against specialty high-fat formulas in acute respiratory failure.

87
00:15:55.998 --> 00:16:07.897
Malnutrition weakens the diaphragm; restarting nutrition in a starved patient can trigger refeeding syndrome with hypophosphatemia.

88
00:16:07.897 --> 00:16:10.258
Nutrition affects weaning.
